sending a child off to college can be  tough especially if it's your only or  last child living at home so what can  you do to prepare yourself for those  changes here to talk about empty nest  syndrome and how to prepare for it is  psychiatrist timothy yeider with nevada  mental health dr eider thanks for being  here today thanks for having me kendall  it is tough when you have a child for 18  years and suddenly boom they're an adult  they're gone and especially if it's the  only child or the last one that you're  sending off what is empty nest syndrome  so it is exactly what you're just  describing is when there's a general  sense of reduction in the number of  people in the household but very  particularly when the last kid has left  the home and there's now this large  dynamic shift in the home because where  there used to be three people now  there's only two and that's just a huge  shift for people yeah and so what is the  emotional impact for parents  the the two largest things we tend to  see is when you have a parent who's been  a full-time parent right their whole  daily routine is around taking care of  their kid making sure their needs are  met and that routine is now disrupted  what do they do with their time the  other aspect is when that kid may have  served as a buffer in kind of a  contentious relationship where there may  have been some problems but it's easier  to focus on the kid rather than the  problem and now that buffer's gone  reality sets in right now you can focus  on each other it can be intense so what  can be done to mitigate some of those  things so the for the first group you're  going to look at finding new purpose new  meaning right we do great and feel great  when we're positively contributing to  our community and our society so find  new purpose find new ways to be involved  for the other set if you're noticing hey  now that buffer is gone there's those  really awkward nights the silences maybe  there's some relationship issues get  counseling a little bit of counseling  can go a long way to help adjust to that  period and save yourselves a lot of  headache in the long run and also you  don't want to inadvertently create  anxiety for the child you're sending  away  and have them worried about you right i  mean that's that's the we see kids come  into our practice all the time with that  where they've gone off to college and  they come home on break and hey mom mom  and dad are my parents aren't really the  same what's going on and then they get a  divorce and now that kid's thinking what  did i do how did this impact like what  did i do to this situation so then they  internalize that and so it can impact  the whole family counseling is always  good and some people are resistant to it  but i think the any stigma attached to  it seems to be diminishing it it has in  the last few years so many more people  have been open about  taking care of themselves including  mental health and so when when public  figures talk out about the services  they've had it really helps the rest of  us normalize the experience because i  can guarantee you everybody's had  something at some point that they've  needed help your clinic is particularly  busy uh in the wake of the pandemic yeah  that's the other big shifts right you  you throw everyone together in a small  space for large periods of time kids  aren't going to school they're home  parents are working from there again  that was a huge shift that no one saw  coming which created a lot of issues and  thankfully a lot of folks have reached  out for help well we appreciate you  being here today dr yider thanks so much  good to be here thank you for having me  all right rocky thank you
 

